Problem: the 'déploiement' (unfolding) of a stratified space. X is a space stratified by strata X_i indexed by an ordered set, with tubular neighbourhoods Tub(X_k, X_i). The page shows how to cut X into compact pieces with corners, indexed by chains of strata, and how X is recovered from the diagram of these pieces. This is the programme announced in Esquisse d'un programme, §5–6.

(1) Picture (Σ_2 = a disc). A point stratum Σ_0 sits at the centre, a 1-dimensional stratum Σ_1 runs out of it, and the open 2-stratum surrounds both. After removing open tubes:
- Σ_{02} is the circle bounding the tube of the point inside the 2-stratum;
- Σ_{01} (also written C_{01}) is the link of 0 in stratum 1;
- Σ_{12} is the pair of parallel lines bounding the tube of Σ_1 inside the 2-stratum;
- Σ_{012} are the corner points where these meet.
The small graph Γ at top right seems to record incidences or links between strata 0, 1, 2.

(2) General definition. To each chain i_0 < … < i_n one attaches Σ_{i_0…i_n}. Reading the crossed text as 'foncteur contravariant par rapport aux diagrammes variables (i_0,…,i_n)', Σ is a contravariant functor on the category of chains (the subdivision of the poset).
- Σ_i = X'_i = X_i \ (X_i ∩ ⋃_{k<i} T̊ub(X_k, X_i)): the stratum with open tubes of lower strata removed, a manifold with corners.
- Σ_{(i,j)} = Tub°(X'_i, X_j) \ ⋃_{i<k<j} T̊ub(X_k, X_j) ↪ X'_j = Σ_j. This is the boundary (sphere-bundle) part of the tube of X'_i in X_j, with intermediate tubes removed. It is a boundary face of Σ_j and fibres over Σ_i.

(3) Second, homotopical version. Using neighbourhoods V instead of tubes, with X*_i the stratum:
- V_{ij} = V(X*_i, X_j) \ ⋃_{i<k<j} (V(X*_i, X_j) ∩ X_k) ↪ X*_j, with V_{ij} → X*_i;
- a diagram V_{ijk} → V_{jk} ← X*_k over V_{ij} → V(X*_j, X_k)…, V_{ij} → X*_i.
This is a system of 'link' spaces with composition-like maps for triples i<j<k. It is essentially the data of homotopy links and the exit-path category of the stratification.

(4) Margin. A square of W̊^{d} spaces indexed by sub-flags d ⊂ i_0 appears, consistent with functoriality in flags.

(5) Next page. Grothendieck axiomatizes the three-stratum case abstractly:
- Σ_{ij} ⊂ Σ_i × Σ_j closed, with Σ_{012} = Σ_{01} ×_{Σ_1} Σ_{12};
- cartesian squares;
- 'bandant' (collared boundary) embeddings and transversality;
- fibration conditions.
The aim is a combinatorial-geometric category of 'unfolded' systems equivalent to tame stratified spaces.
